11
00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:16.480
Mengjia Wei: The reason we selected this paper, is that XAI methodology is very trendy right now, it's highly practical, it has been, adopted by many big, banks, for example, JP Morgan.

12
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:26.230
Mengjia Wei: It provides data scientists a tool to move from simply saying what a model predicts to understanding the logic and the feature that drove the output.

13
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:33.980
Mengjia Wei: Before we get into XOI, I want to establish why it exists in the first place.

14
00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:46.119
Mengjia Wei: If we think about… think back about all the models that we've been, using and learned in our data science courses, they can be essentially grouped into white box models or black box models.

15
00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:53.960
Mengjia Wei: Models include, logist regression, linear regression, decision trees, these are what we call white box models.

16
00:01:54.090 --> 00:02:00.399
Mengjia Wei: Because the… these models' output are completely visible and very easy to explain.

17
00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:16.739
Mengjia Wei: For example, with logistic regression, every coefficient directly tells us how much a feature shifts the prediction. With a decision tree, we can trace any prediction from the root node all the way to the leaf.

18
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.020
Mengjia Wei: And read the logic step-by-step.

19
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:36.179
Mengjia Wei: These models can explain themselves pretty well, no actual interpretation needed. In contrast, if you look at the right hand, neural networks and ensemble methods, like XGBoost and Random Forest, are what we call black box models.

20
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:40.859
Mengjia Wei: Because the behavior of the model are not transparent.

21
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:48.890
Mengjia Wei: A neural network, for example, might have millions of weights distributed across the hidden layers and neurons.

22
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.559
Mengjia Wei: There is no human readable path from input to the output.

23
00:02:54.060 --> 00:03:01.370
Mengjia Wei: You read the data, you get a prediction, but the internal computation is completely opaque.

24
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:21.169
Mengjia Wei: Xgboost works similarly, even though we can finally, let models return the tree, but even though individual trees, pass, there's a path for us to trace individual trees, but the entire, ensemble as a whole is not visible.

25
00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:29.119
Mengjia Wei: And here is the core tension that XAI is built around. It provides data scientists a workflow to follow.

26
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:36.929
Mengjia Wei: In order to select the right method to use, to shed light on the behaviors of the black box model's output.

27
00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:46.479
Mengjia Wei: Before getting into, the XAI, I do want to explain, why the accuracy in, alone is not enough.

28
00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:56.070
Mengjia Wei: So we might be able to build a model and achieve a 96% accuracy score, for example, but we can still be wrong with the explanation.

29
00:03:56.310 --> 00:04:13.619
Mengjia Wei: If we look at this metrics and focus on the right top corner, this is the critical zone which defines an explanation of the model output. Imagine we were a data scientist helping with a cancer diagnosis, working in the medical diagnosis area.

30
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:31.199
Mengjia Wei: In addition to predicting whether a cell is a cancer cell, we should also provide a supportive explanation. For example, we can say this cell is flagged as a cancer cell because of its irregular border.

31
00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.830
Mengjia Wei: So…

32
00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:47.160
Mengjia Wei: that's, that's, that's where the XAI, comes along. So if we look at the, the role of XAI and where it sits in the MLOps model.

33
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:57.270
Mengjia Wei: This is a much simplified MLOps diagram. What we have been practicing as a data scientist is in the blue area.

34
00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:15.069
Mengjia Wei: That's something we've been very familiar with. We, collect the data, feed the model with the data, run the model, train the model, tune the model, and, come up with a prediction. And we can finally select a model that gives us the best predictive score.

35
00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:32.090
Mengjia Wei: Then the XAI comes along, which is colored in the yellow area, after we've already finished the model building. We feed XAI with the new data, and then we try to use other methods to explain

36
00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:38.240
Mengjia Wei: The output of the black box model in order to shed some light, for the explanation.

37
00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:47.249
Mengjia Wei: Taxonomy of XLI techniques, this is probably one of the most important things developed by this literature.

38
00:05:47.250 --> 00:06:04.399
Mengjia Wei: So essentially, what this table tells us is that, the other groups, all these methods you can see here into, by three dimensions. So the first dimension is, are we trying to do global interpretation or local interpretation?

39
00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:18.840
Mengjia Wei: Global interpretation means that we try to explain the whole model behavior using the entire dataset. For example, we try to understand what features drive the output and what features are highly correlated to the prediction, by how much.

40
00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:30.869
Mengjia Wei: Local interpretation, on the other hand, means we try to develop, we try to explain, one single prediction. For example, why the tree makes specific split.

41
00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:41.100
Mengjia Wei: The second dimension is white box versus black box, something we've discussed, meaning whether the model behavior is transparent or not.

42
00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:55.980
Mengjia Wei: The third dimension is, whether the model… whether the method is model-dependent or not. Model-dependent means that the method… certain methods can only be used along with certain models, like neural networks or trees model.

43
00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:01.599
Mengjia Wei: Model agnostic means that this method can be used with any types of models.

44
00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:22.299
Mengjia Wei: For the sake of the time, we are unable to explain all of them, but we do… Choi and I are going to cover, on a high level, some of the methods that is being highly discussed in this literature. So the first, method I want to cover is the integrated gradients.

45
00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:37.759
Mengjia Wei: So what this does is that, so first of all, integrated gradients can only be used with neural networks, especially with the, pixel-level image data, or the token-level natural language processing data.

46
00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:44.449
Mengjia Wei: So what this does is that it tries to assign each feature's importance using a score.

47
00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:55.200
Mengjia Wei: If you look at the example here at the bottom, this is an image, and the neural network model predicts this image to be a shirt with a certain accuracy.

48
00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:15.540
Mengjia Wei: Integrated gradients, so what integrated gradient does is that it tries to tell us on the pixel level, on the image, whether they positively or negatively drive toward the decision from the model output.

49
00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:30.300
Mengjia Wei: So if you look at this collar area and the sleeves area here, those pixels are colored in green, means that these pixels contribute positively toward the prediction of the model.

50
00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:42.429
Mengjia Wei: But the torso area in the middle are colored in pink, means these pixels are driving the prediction to… it drives negatively toward the prediction.

51
00:08:42.429 --> 00:09:00.770
Mengjia Wei: The rationale behind that is that collar and sleeves are very correlated with the shape of the shirt, but the middle area is not necessarily related to the shirt, it is also… it can also be part of the jeans or trousers.

52
00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:04.339
Mengjia Wei: So this is the idea of integrated gradient.

53
00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:18.049
Mengjia Wei: The second method that I want to, review, is SHOP. I'm sure a lot of us have already heard about it or used about it. SHOP, stands for Sharply Addictive Explanation.

54
00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:31.600
Mengjia Wei: So, the idea is similar as integrated gradient, but it… from a different, level or perspective. So, what Sharp gave us is…

55
00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:38.680
Mengjia Wei: For each feature, it tells the feature's marginal contribution toward the prediction.

56
00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:45.299
Mengjia Wei: So for example, if you look at this example on the right hand, this is a loan denial case.

57
00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:53.150
Mengjia Wei: So the model predicts that there is 85% of chance we should deny this, applicant's loan application.

58
00:09:53.870 --> 00:10:02.970
Mengjia Wei: So, the models start with a baseline of 0.5, means there's 50% either, approve or deny this case.

59
00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:09.350
Mengjia Wei: Sharp returns sharply value for all the, important features.

60
00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:13.270
Mengjia Wei: For example, that ratio.

61
00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:21.230
Mengjia Wei: Drives the prediction up by 20, by 22 points toward, denial.

62
00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:30.270
Mengjia Wei: Credit score drives the prediction up by 14 points toward denial. Loan amount drives it up by 8 points.

63
00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:41.870
Mengjia Wei: Even though income at age drives the prediction down toward approval, a little bit, but isn't able to cancel the effect from the other factors.

64
00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:56.599
Mengjia Wei: So, these are the Shapley values. If we add these values together, it comes up to 35 points, and if we add it back to the .5, it comes up to 85. So that's how the .85 is calculated.

65
00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:05.490
Mengjia Wei: Okay, so now I'm gonna hand it over to Choi to introduce other methods, discussed in this article.

66
00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:14.859
Yu Cheng: Okay, so for the third method we want to introduce is Lime. It's a more simpler and more lightweight alternative.

67
00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:33.020
Yu Cheng: So Lime is short for Local Interpretable Model Agnostic Explanations. Like, SHARP explains individual predictions, but where SHARP uses game theory, LIME uses a much simpler idea. If you want to understand…

68
00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:46.240
Yu Cheng: How a black box model behaves near one specific data point. Build a simple interpretable model, a linear regression, that approximates the black box in that local neighborhood.

69
00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:56.849
Yu Cheng: The process has four steps. So first, take the one data point you want to expand, take the sharp example, say, one specific loan applicant.

70
00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:11.560
Yu Cheng: And second, perturb the data point hundreds of times, slightly vary the age, the income, and the debt ratio, and run age-perturbed version through the black box model to collect predictions.

71
00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:24.729
Yu Cheng: And third, fade a model linear model on those perturbed samples, weighting them by how similar they are to the original data point. And fourth.

72
00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:34.359
Yu Cheng: The last step, read the coefficients of that linear model. Those are the feature contributions for that single prediction.

73
00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:45.720
Yu Cheng: And we can also use this process, work through this cat picture example. So first, we start with the original image. In this case, it's a cat.

74
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:58.700
Yu Cheng: Then line breaks the image into interpretable regions of pixels. This is a segmentation step, so instead of looking at individual pixels.

75
00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:06.299
Yu Cheng: it groups them into meaningful parts of the image. For example, the face, the background, and the body.

76
00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:20.179
Yu Cheng: Next comes, the most important step, perturbation, perturbation. Lime creates many slightly modified versions of the image by turning some regions on and off.

77
00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:26.690
Yu Cheng: Then it faces variations into the model and observes how this prediction changes.

78
00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:34.009
Yu Cheng: So, essentially, it is testing, if I remove this part of the image, does the prediction change?

79
00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:36.210
Yu Cheng: Finally, line phase,

80
00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:44.610
Yu Cheng: Simple local linear model to approximate the behavior of black box model around the specific image.

81
00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:57.389
Yu Cheng: The result is what we can see here. Only certain regions are highlighted. These are the parts of the image that contributed most to the prediction of, this is a cat.

82
00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:09.010
Yu Cheng: So the key idea is Lyme doesn't try to understand the whole model. It only expands this one decision by approximating the model locally.

83
00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:17.469
Yu Cheng: compared to the method of shock, Lime is faster, and it works on any model. It's not model-dependent.

84
00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:35.849
Yu Cheng: But it also has two significant weaknesses. First, stability. If you run line twice on the same instance, you may get different feature weights, because the neighborhood is defined arbitrarily, and the sampling introduces randomness.

85
00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:50.680
Yu Cheng: So, the results is kind of unstable. Secondly, it offers no additivity guarantee. So, like, unlike Sharp, its weights don't necessarily sound to anything meaningful, just like the

86
00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:53.340
Yu Cheng: 85 thing.

87
00:14:53.930 --> 00:15:10.710
Yu Cheng: So, the practical rule is use Lime for quick for the ADA stage, interactive exploration, when we are trying to get a rough sense of what's driving the prediction, and use Sharp for anything you are going to report

88
00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:19.349
Yu Cheng: to a stakeholder or attend an audit trial. SHARP is a stable and mathematically guaranteed. Lime is not.

89
00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:21.459
Yu Cheng: That's… that's life, please.

90
00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.290
Yu Cheng: So,

91
00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:46.189
Yu Cheng: We also got other explanation methods. Here is the PDP, ICE, and ALE, and we are gonna make a very simple comparison of these three methods. So let's start with the PDP on the left.

92
00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:50.269
Yu Cheng: The PDP is short for Partial Dependence Plots.

93
00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:53.370
Yu Cheng: It is a global explanation method.

94
00:15:53.510 --> 00:16:01.450
Yu Cheng: shows the average effect of a feature on the model's predictions. So here, on the x-axis.

95
00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:21.729
Yu Cheng: We have the feature value, and on the y-axis, we have the predicted outcome. The curve tells us, as the feature's value increases, how does the prediction change on average? So PDP gives us a clean, simple global trend, but it is only an average. However, PDP has a

96
00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:25.539
Yu Cheng: very important limitation. It assumes that

97
00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:31.690
Yu Cheng: All the features are independent, which is often not true in the real-world data, right?

98
00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:45.089
Yu Cheng: So, while PDP is useful for understanding general trends, we need to be cautious because features are highly correlated. So the key takeaway of this PDP method is

99
00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.830
Yu Cheng: it can answer this question. So, how does this…

100
00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:59.010
Yu Cheng: feature effect predictions overall. It's an average sense of this feature's effect on the predictions, on the final outcome.

101
00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:14.120
Yu Cheng: And now we can move to the middle, the ICE plot. The ICE means individual condition… conditional expectation. We can see what's actually behind the average.

102
00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:32.479
Yu Cheng: Instead of the single curve, like PDP, I see it shows a one line per data… per data point. So each line represents how the prediction changes for a specific instance. And now we can say something important. Not all data points behave the same way.

103
00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:51.109
Yu Cheng: Some lines goes down sharply, and others stay relatively flat. So, ICE reveals the heterogeneity that PDP hides. PDP shows the average, but ICE shows that variation. It's a detailed version of PDP.

104
00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:54.500
Yu Cheng: And finally, on the right, we have ALE method.

105
00:17:54.780 --> 00:18:01.509
Yu Cheng: which is accumulated local effects. You can think of ALE as an improved version of PDP.

106
00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:21.000
Yu Cheng: The key differences is ALE doesn't assume the features were independent. In real-world data, features are often correlated, and PDP can give misleading results in that case. ALE corrects this by marrying local changes instead of global averages.

107
00:18:21.380 --> 00:18:33.319
Yu Cheng: So it gives me more reliable global explanation when features interact with each other. It's more robust in realistic settings.

